as communities continue to work to  prevent new surges of covid including  from the fast spreading omicron variant  i've heard the call from chiefs and  communities for faster access to  financial resources to prepare for  outbreaks implement additional public  health measures and respond quickly to  covenanting cases to prevent widespread  infection  so to help with those efforts indigenous  services canada is flowing 125 million  of cova 19 public health funding  directly to first nations self-governing  and modern treaty holders across the  country to support their ongoing public  health efforts  this additional financial support will  help first nations in their efforts to  enhance their community surge capacity  to protect against covenanting  infections  some examples of how the money may be  used could include to administer  vaccines and boosters or to conduct  rapid testing it could also be used to  pay for surge staffing and other  resources food security or other  supports for people in isolation or  updating reviewing and activating  pandemic response plans  the funding can also be used in a  variety of other flexible means in a way  that communities determine that will  best protect their members  speed of support matters when a  community is under threat of an outbreak  and we will continue to support  indigenous leaders and communities as  they work to protect their members  the money will be allocated using the  indigenous community support fund  methodology and will include an  allocation to the first nations health  authority in british columbia the  allocation and its temporary  implementation criteria have also  included careful condition  considerations for needs of remote and  isolated first nations  the indigenous community support fund  also remains available to indigenous  communities and organizations this  needs-based fund gives first nations  inuit and metis communities and  organizations the flexibility to design  and implement community-based solutions  so that they can prevent prepare or  respond to the spread of coven 19 within  their communities  indigenous service canada regional  offices and regional medical officers of  health across the country remain  available to assist first nations inuit  and metis communities and organizations  if they need  get help with an outbreak or support  such as temporary infrastructure rapid  testing or ppe as of january the 18th  over a million  vaccine doses have been administered in  first nations inuit and territorial  communities and of those  over 85 percent of individuals aged 12  and up have received a second dose  only about 20 percent of adults have  received a booster dose  and over 40 percent of children aged 5  to 11 have received at least one dose  as of january the 19th there are 597  active cases in first nations  communities  sadly there have been a total of 588  fatalities with kovit since the  beginning of the pandemic
 

